 d("Narrate in your own words how Tricki recovered at the surgery.", "Tricki arrived fat, sluggish and unwilling to eat. Herriot kept him in the kennels and gave him a simple diet. (2) At first Tricki was lonely, but he soon met other dogs who played with him. Seeing them eat, he joined in, and his lost appetite returned. Daily exercise slimmed him down and brought back his energy. (2) After a few days he was a lively, healthy dog again and was ready to go home to his delighted owner. (1)"),
 d("'Excess of love can harm.' Discuss this idea with reference to the story.", "Mrs Pumphrey's affection for Tricki was genuine but excessive. She fed him cakes, chocolates and cream whenever he looked at her, and she never let him run about. (2) As a result, he became breathless, fat and ill, though she believed she was doing him good. Herriot's firm but kind treatment shows what real care means: proper food, exercise and company. (2) The story therefore teaches that love needs wisdom, otherwise it becomes a harmful indulgence. (1)"),
