 d("Why is Herriot's way of dealing with Mrs Pumphrey described as tactful?", "Herriot realised that she loved her dog deeply and would be hurt if told she was to blame. Rather than accuse her, he proposed that Tricki stay at the surgery for a rest. (2) He kept the real reason of his illness gentle and let her imagine that the dog was getting special care. When Tricki recovered, he accepted her thanks gracefully. (2) This approach saved her pride, cured the dog and kept a good client, showing the value of tact in dealing with people. (1)"),
 d("Describe the setting and characters of the story in a short essay.", "The story is set in a small Yorkshire town where Herriot works as an assistant to the vet Mr Farnon. (2) The main characters are Herriot, the thoughtful narrator; Mr Farnon, his plain-speaking employer; Mrs Pumphrey, the rich pet-lover; and Tricki, the spoilt Pekingese. The chauffeur and the kennel dogs add colour. (2) The calm, rural background suits the gentle, humorous tone of the narrative. (1)"),
 d("Write a diary entry of Herriot on the day Tricki went home.", "Date and heading to begin the entry. (2) Mention how the lively Tricki leapt about the surgery, wagged his tail and ate his plain dinner eagerly, thanks to the other dogs. Describe Mrs Pumphrey's tears of joy and her talk of 'surgery'. (2) End with a quiet remark that it was nature, not surgery, that had cured the dog, and a hope that the lady will now be wiser. (1)"),
 d("Compare Tricki's life at home with his life at the surgery.", "At home Tricki lived in luxury: soft cushions, endless sweets and a mistress who obeyed his every whim. He had no exercise, so he grew dull and fat. (2) At the surgery he was fed plain food, made to move about and allowed to play with other dogs. There he became fit, active and happy. (2) The comparison shows that comfort without discipline is harmful, while simple living with companionship brings health. (1)"),
 d("Write a short speech for school assembly on 'Pets need care, not pampering'.", "Begin with a respectful greeting to the principal, teachers and friends. Introduce the topic by mentioning Tricki, the pampered dog. (2) Explain that love for pets must be sensible: proper food, exercise and company are more important than sweets and luxury. Show how Tricki fell ill because of over-feeding. (2) Close with an appeal to treat animals kindly but wisely, and thank the audience. (1)"),
 d("What message does the author wish to convey through this humorous story?", "The author wishes to say that affection must be combined with common sense. Mrs Pumphrey's love for Tricki became harmful because it ignored the dog's real needs. (2) Simple remedies such as exercise, proper diet and company can cure what luxury has spoilt. The story also shows that a good professional must be tactful and honest at once. (2) These lessons are delivered in a light and amusing manner. (1)"),
